{#
	The wrapper every email this application sends is drawn in (XIV-38, §5.13).

	**It ships in code and a tenant cannot edit it**, which is §6.1's existing rule
	rather than a new one: presets live in code, templates live in data. A customer
	writes the content part — the words, in Markdown — and this is the skeleton
	around it. Somebody who could edit this could break every email they send, and
	the skeleton is not the thing they wanted to change anyway.

	**There is one of it**, not a named set, and the argument is in §5.13: nothing
	yet needs two, and a second would have to be chosen from — a column on
	email_template whose only value would be "the default", plus a picker beside a
	field somebody actually cares about.

	**Written the way email is written, not the way a page is.** Inline styles,
	because Gmail and Outlook both drop a <style> block often enough that relying on
	one is relying on luck; a table for the frame, because Outlook lays out with
	Word's engine and a centred div is not something it agrees to. This is not the
	house Bootstrap style and it is not meant to be — the audience is a mail client
	from 2009 as much as a modern one.

	`content` arrives as HTML that has already been through CommonMark and the
	sanitizer (see EmailRenderer), which is why it is the one thing here printed
	raw. Everything else on this page is escaped by Twig as usual.
#}
<!doctype html>
<html>
	<head>
		<meta charset="utf-8">
		<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
		<title>{{ subject }}</title>
	</head>
	<body style="margin: 0; padding: 0; background-color: #f4f5f7; color: #212529; font-family: -apple-system, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Helvetica, Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.6;">
		<table role="presentation" width="100%" cellpadding="0" cellspacing="0" border="0" style="background-color: #f4f5f7;">
			<tr>
				<td align="center" style="padding: 24px 12px;">
					<table role="presentation" width="600" cellpadding="0" cellspacing="0" border="0" style="width: 100%; max-width: 600px; background-color: #ffffff; border-radius: 8px;">
						<tr>
							<td style="padding: 32px 32px 8px 32px;">
								{#
									The content part, and the only part a customer wrote.
									Raw because it is already sanitized HTML rather than
									text — escaping it here would print the tags.
								#}
								{{ content|raw }}
							</td>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<td style="padding: 8px 32px 32px 32px;">
								<hr style="border: 0; border-top: 1px solid #dee2e6; margin: 16px 0 12px 0;">
								{#
									Who this came from, which the engine cannot know: it
									arrives as a general marker through DocumentContext,
									the same seam a document's [tenant.name] comes
									through (§5.7). Who the message is *from* in the
									headers is a different question and XIV-37's.
								#}
								<p style="margin: 0; color: #6c757d; font-size: 13px; line-height: 1.5;">
									{{ general['tenant.name']|default('') }}
								</p>
							</td>
						</tr>
					</table>
				</td>
			</tr>
		</table>
	</body>
</html>
